  "latest_cpp_test_scope": "The C++ scenario encodes and decodes HLAfixedArray and HLAvariableArray instances containing a nested HLAfixedRecord, checking nested values and containing-array size. It also round-trips an HLAfixedRecord containing a simple HLAoctet and a nested HLAfixedRecord. Fixed- and variable-array decode checks use prepopulated targets and verify decoded values/count replace the prior state; variable-array cases include empty input, stale-element clearing, and reducing a three-element receiver to exactly two encoded elements. Typed HLAfixedArrayT<HLAoctet> and HLAvariableArrayT<HLAoctet> decodes replace seeded contents; typed HLAvariantRecordT<HLAinteger32BE> decodes both simple and nested-complex alternatives into receivers initially selecting the opposite alternative. The untyped HLAvariantRecord and HLAextendableVariantRecord round trips likewise check opposite-alternative replacement. These selected compositions strengthen existing p. 381 l. 4-1 element-types, l. 22-7 variant, l. 25-8 size, and p. 382 l. 18-5 typed-element witnesses only for the tested shapes; they do not assert Java parity or every possible complex-element combination.",
  "notes": "The C++ scenario has compile-time std::is_class assertions naming the five non-templated utility classes and three standard templated utility classes in the exact rti1516_2025 namespace, in addition to its runtime API evidence. Focused public C++ TCK evidence covers IEEE 1516.1-2025 §12.12.4.2 p. 381 l. 4-1, its element-types continuation, HLAvariableArray.size at l. 25-8, addElement/get(index) at l. 10-3 and l. 13-4, and discriminant-selected variant composition/encoding/decoding at l. 22-7. The Java counterpart covers those six p. 381 requirements using the Java generic API: simple and nested-complex constituents, variable-array addElement/size/get, fixed-record access, and simple/nested discriminant-selected variant round trips. The Java case does not claim the C++-specific operator[], addVariant name, HLAfixedArrayT/HLAvariableArrayT/HLAvariantRecordT class names, or p. 382 template requirements. The C++ scenario additionally instantiates those templated classes and verifies typed element and discriminant access. The separate l. 16-5 addMapping wording conflict is tracked in RL-420 and is not mapped by this contract. This is selected API evidence, not an exhaustive claim about every possible encoder or Annex C conformance.",
